mormot2-qwen-tts
================

THIRD-PARTY COMPONENTS AND REFERENCES

mORMot 2  (Synopse Informatique - Arnaud Bouchez)
  Runtime foundation: JSON, RTTI, variants, threads, logging, tests.
  License: MPL 1.1 / GPL 2.0 / LGPL 2.1 (three-license).
  https://github.com/synopse/mORMot2

mormot2-ai - mormot.ai.* extension  (LandrixAI - Landrix / Sven Harazim)
  The native inference engine this work is built on: safetensors loader, BPE
  tokenizer, AVX2 kernels, thread pool, sampler, the decoder that runs the
  talker and the code predictor on the CPU or the GPU (TDecoderModel,
  TQwen3Vulkan), Vulkan device and shaders; its test unit test.infer.ref
  reads the dataset of tests/data/infer-ref. Used as the git submodule
  external/mormot2-ai, not copied; tools/tts/vk/convgemm.comp follows the
  tiling of its gemm8.comp.
  License: MPL 1.1 / GPL 2.0 / LGPL 2.1 (three-license, follows mORMot).
  https://github.com/landrix/mormot2-ai

Qwen3-TTS  (Alibaba Cloud - Qwen team)  (FOLLOWED: architecture and prompt)
  The engine computes Qwen/Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-0.6B-CustomVoice (revision
  85e237c12c027371202489a0ec509ded67b5e4b5): the talker, the code predictor
  and the 12 Hz speech tokenizer decoder, reimplemented in Pascal and GLSL
  after the qwen_tts package 0.1.1 (core/models/modeling_qwen3_tts.py,
  core/tokenizer_12hz/modeling_qwen3_tts_tokenizer_v2.py,
  inference/qwen3_tts_model.py). No Python code and no weights are copied or
  shipped; the model is downloaded by the user.
  The dataset tests/data/infer-ref/qwen3-tts-lm holds values computed by this
  model in PyTorch, and 14 rows of its codec embeddings.
  License of the model and of qwen_tts: Apache-2.0.
  https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-0.6B-CustomVoice
  https://github.com/QwenLM/Qwen3-TTS

Kokoro-82M  (hexgrad)  (FOLLOWED: architecture, voices, vocabulary)
  The Kokoro engine computes hexgrad/Kokoro-82M (revision
  f3ff3571791e39611d31c381e3a41a3af07b4987): ALBERT, the prosody predictor,
  the text encoder, the decoder and the iSTFTNet vocoder, reimplemented in
  Pascal after the kokoro package 0.9.4 (model.py, modules.py, istftnet.py;
  the language codes of pipeline.py). No Python code and no weights are
  copied or shipped; the model is downloaded by the user.
  tools/tts/kokoro_ref.py drives the kokoro package to write the test
  references.
  License of the model and of kokoro: Apache-2.0.
  https://huggingface.co/hexgrad/Kokoro-82M
  https://github.com/hexgrad/kokoro

StyleTTS 2  (Yinghao Aaron Li et al.)  (FOLLOWED: architecture)
  Kokoro is a StyleTTS 2 model with an iSTFTNet decoder; its modules (AdaIN
  residual blocks, the duration encoder, the harmonic source, the Snake
  residual blocks of the generator) come from the StyleTTS 2 code.
  License: MIT.
  https://github.com/yl4579/StyleTTS2

misaki  (hexgrad)  (FOLLOWED: G2P post-processing)
  mormot.ai.tts.espeak applies the phoneme replacements of misaki 0.9.4's
  EspeakG2P (its e2m table, the handling of quotes and parentheses), so that
  the phonemes match what Kokoro was trained on.
  License: Apache-2.0.
  https://github.com/hexgrad/misaki

phonemizer  (Mathieu Bernard, CoML)  (FOLLOWED: punctuation handling)
  mormot.ai.tts.espeak reproduces, step by step, the behaviour of
  phonemizer's Punctuation.preserve / restore and of the EspeakBackend line
  post-processing (phonemizer-fork 3.3.2, as used by misaki), so that espeak
  receives the same pieces of text and the output is joined back the same
  way. No Python code is shipped.
  License: GPL-3.0-or-later.
  https://github.com/bootphon/phonemizer

espeak-ng  (RUNTIME ONLY)
  mormot.ai.tts.espeak loads espeak-ng.dll (or libespeak-ng) at runtime from
  a folder given by the user, and calls espeak_TextToPhonemes. espeak-ng is
  neither shipped, nor linked at build time, nor needed to build.
  License: GPL-3.0-or-later.
  https://github.com/espeak-ng/espeak-ng

PyTorch serialization format  (FOLLOWED: file format)
  mormot.ai.tts.torchfile reads the zip + pickle files written by
  torch.save (protocols 2 to 5, the _rebuild_tensor_v2 / *Storage objects).
  https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch

Cephes Mathematical Library  (Stephen L. Moshier)  (FOLLOWED: coefficients)
  tools/tts/vk/snake.comp and KokoroSin (mormot.ai.tts.kokoro.kernels)
  evaluate sin and cos with the minimax polynomial coefficients of the Cephes
  sinf/cosf routines, after a Cody-Waite range reduction. Only the published
  coefficients are used.
  https://www.netlib.org/cephes/

Abramowitz & Stegun, Handbook of Mathematical Functions, 7.1.26
  tools/tts/vk/convgemm.comp computes erf (for the exact GELU) with this
  published rational approximation.

glslang  (Khronos Group)  (TOOL ONLY)
  tools/tts/vk/build-shaders.ps1 compiles the shaders with the official
  glslang release into src/tts/mormot.ai.tts.qwen.spv.inc; glslang is neither
  shipped nor needed to build or run.
  https://github.com/KhronosGroup/glslang
